        "programSlug": "captain",
        "week": 1,
        "answers": [
          {
            "question": "What is the difference between a Lieutenant's and a Captain's scope of responsibility?",
            "answer": "A Lieutenant typically serves as a tactical, mid-level manager responsible for daily shift operations, supervising units (such as a platoon or police watch), and executing broader directives. A Captain holds a broader senior-level role, commanding an entire company, station, or specific division while focusing on overall administration, strategic planning, and personnel management"
          },
          {
            "question": "What does 'managing infrastructure' mean versus 'managing projects'?",
            "answer": "Managing infrastructure means maintaining the foundational, \"always-on\" systems (like servers, networks, or roadways) that keep an organization or society running daily. Managing projects involves leading temporary, one-off endeavors with specific scopes and deadlines (like developing a new app or building a bridge) to create a unique deliverable"
          },
          {
            "question": "Draw a simple organizational chart for your functional area. Who reports to you? Who do you report to?",
            "answer": "Levies report to CPL, CPL to SGT, SGT to SFC, SFC to Knight Aspirant, Knight Aspirant to Knight, Knight to Knight LT., Knight LT to Knight Captain, Knight CPT to Knight Major, Knight Major to Knight Commander\n\nNon NCOs report to Temple Master, Temple Masters report to Master of Temple\nTemple Turcos report to High Turco\nTemple Marshal reports to High Marshal\nTemple Seneschal to High Seneschal\nTemple Almoner reports to High Almoner\nTemple Chaplains reports to the Asst. and Master Chaplain\nWomen's  Corps reports to High Guardian\nHSY reports to High Marshal and GrandMaster\nEveryone reports to GrandMaster"
          }
        ],
